<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/intermediate/logic-gates-and-digital-circuits">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    You need the foundational lesson Binary and Bits behind you — binary numbers and
    Boolean values are the raw material this lesson wires into machines. Programming
    Fundamentals is useful company but not required. The route builds from five gates to
    arithmetic to memory, ending at the abstraction stack your processor runs on.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>write the truth tables for AND, OR, XOR, NAND and NOR from memory;</li>
    <li>translate between Boolean expressions, circuits and truth tables in both directions;</li>
    <li>apply De Morgan's laws, and simplify an expression before building it;</li>
    <li>explain how a half adder computes a sum, and how feedback turns gates into memory;</li>
    <li>argue why NAND alone can build every circuit, and why binary wins against noise.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Between the binary numbers lesson and this one sits the physical miracle:
    <strong>bits are voltages, and logic gates are transistors arranged to compute</strong>.
    An AND gate is a few transistors wired so that its output wire sits high only when
    both input wires sit high; an OR, when either does. That is all a "gate" is — a tiny
    voltage-controlling circuit with a contract. Claude Shannon proved in his 1937
    master's thesis that these circuits implement Boolean algebra, the logic George Boole
    had published in 1854 — meaning a machine could compute <em>anything expressible in
    logic</em> — and the digital age followed from that one observation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The syllabus unit has three layers, and this lesson keeps them in order:
    <strong>logic gates</strong> (the components and their contracts), <strong>truth
    tables</strong> (the mathematics that specifies and verifies them), and <strong>logic
    circuits and expressions</strong> (building circuits from Boolean expressions and
    reading circuits back into expressions) — culminating in the two constructions that
    make a computer a computer: arithmetic from gates, and memory from feedback.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="five gates to know — ending with the one that builds everything" widget="equationStep" config={gateCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the five gates and read each one's
    contract as a sentence: flip it; both must agree; one is enough; they must differ; and
    then the miracle gate. Notice that NAND appears last on purpose — the fifth panel is
    the one the entire chip industry is built on.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> bits are voltages and gates are transistors arranged to
    compute — Shannon's proof that such circuits are Boolean algebra is what made the
    digital age. The first tool for specifying what a circuit must do is its truth table.
  </p>

  <h2>Truth tables: the contract in full</h2>
  <p>
    A gate with n inputs has 2ⁿ possible input combinations, and its truth table lists
    every one with the output — the complete specification, no ambiguity. The two-input
    tables are worth knowing cold. AND: 1 only on row 11. OR: 0 only on row 00. XOR: 1
    on rows 01 and 10 — "exactly one". NAND (NOT-AND): 0 only on row 11. NOR (NOT-OR):
    1 only on row 00. Writing a table by hand is also a verification method: any claim
    about a circuit ("these two wirings are equivalent") becomes checkable row by row —
    and the exponential growth (3 inputs → 8 rows, 4 → 16, 10 → 1,024) is itself the
    reason engineers compress tables back into algebra whenever they can.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> OR means "one or the other, but not both" — that
  A OR B is 0 when both inputs are 1. That description belongs to XOR. The OR gate's
  contract is inclusive: it outputs 1 on every row except 00, including 11. Mixing the
  two up is the single most common truth-table error, and the alarm example shows why it
  matters: smoke AND heat together must still ring the bell.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a truth table is a gate's complete contract, and writing one
    out is itself a verification method. Tables grow exponentially, though, so the next
    tool compresses them back into algebra.
  </p>

  <h2>Boolean algebra: expressions, laws and De Morgan</h2>
  <p>
    Gates compose, and Boolean algebra is the language of the composition: AND written
    as multiplication (A·B or just AB), OR as addition (A+B), NOT as a bar (Ā). The
    laws mirror ordinary algebra where possible (A·1 = A, A+0 = A) and diverge where
    logic is stranger and better: A·A = A, A+A = A (no powers or coefficients in
    logic), A·Ā = 0, A+Ā = 1, and A + A·B = A (if A is true, the second term is
    irrelevant — check it on a two-row table). The most useful identity pair is
    <strong>De Morgan's laws</strong>: NOT(A AND B) = (NOT A) OR (NOT B), and NOT(A OR
    B) = (NOT A) AND (NOT B) — "not both" equals "at least one isn't"; "neither"
    equals "both aren't". Both are provable on a four-row table in seconds, and both
    are everywhere: they are how NAND and NOR can impersonate every other gate, and
    they are the reason "not (logged in and verified)" is identical to "not logged in
    or not verified" in your own security reasoning.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> NOT distributes over AND — that NOT(A AND B) equals
  (NOT A) AND (NOT B). It equals (NOT A) OR (NOT B): negating a conjunction flips it to a
  disjunction, because "not both" and "both are not" are different claims. A quick row
  settles it: A = 1, B = 0 makes NOT(A AND B) true (they are not both 1) while
  (NOT A) AND (NOT B) is false (B is 1). The same trap in English: "not rich and happy"
  is not the same sentence as "not rich and not happy".</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> Boolean algebra gives the composition a language, and De
    Morgan's laws are the identities you will use most. Now the two mechanical directions
    every exam question lives in: expression to circuit, and circuit back to expression.
  </p>

  <h2>From expression to circuit and back</h2>
  <p>
    The two directions of the syllabus's third topic are mechanical once the notation
    is yours. <strong>Expression → circuit</strong>: take (A AND B) OR (NOT C); feed A
    and B into an AND gate; feed C into a NOT; feed the two results into an OR; the OR's
    output is the answer. Every operator becomes a gate, every input a wire, nesting
    becomes depth. <strong>Circuit → expression</strong>: label each gate's output wire
    in turn — the AND emits A·B, the NOT emits C̄, the OR emits A·B + C̄ — and the
    expression falls out of the diagram. Then the payoff step: <strong>simplify before
    building</strong>, because every gate is money. A + A·B simplifies to A (two gates
    to zero); (A AND B) OR (A AND NOT B) simplifies to A (the OR's second term dies
    whenever B is 1 and whenever B is 0 — table it). Real design is this loop: write
    the truth table, derive the expression, simplify with the laws, build the smaller
    circuit, verify the table again.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> simplifying an expression changes what the circuit
  does — that the compact version must be "doing less" than the sprawling one it replaced.
  Boolean simplification produces an <em>equivalent</em> circuit: identical truth table,
  identical behaviour on every input, only fewer gates between them. That guarantee is
  the whole reason the simplification step is safe — and the reason to re-verify with the
  table afterward is habit, not doubt.</p>

  <h2>Arithmetic from gates: the adders</h2>
  <p>
    Addition is where logic becomes computation. The <strong>half adder</strong> adds
    two bits: the sum digit is exactly XOR (1+0 = 1, 0+0 = 0, 1+1 = 0 with a carry),
    and the carry is exactly AND (1 only when both are 1). Two gates, one bit of
    arithmetic. A <strong>full adder</strong> adds the carry coming in from the right
    (two half adders plus an OR — the detail is a standard exercise), and chaining n
    full adders, each carry feeding the next, gives an n-bit adder: 32 of them and the
    machine adds 32-bit integers. This ripple-carry design is deliberately simple and
    deliberately slow (the carry must ripple the full width — the worst case travels
    all 32 stages), which is the honest first draft of a truth real processors refine
    with carry-lookahead logic that computes carries in parallel. The point stands:
    arithmetic is a wiring diagram, and you have now read the smallest honest version
    of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> adders showed that arithmetic is a wiring diagram — XOR for
    the sum digit, AND for the carry. But combinational circuits forget everything the
    moment the inputs change; remembering requires a loop.
  </p>

  <h2>Memory from feedback: latches, clocks, registers</h2>
  <p>
    Combinational circuits (adders, and everything above) have no memory — outputs
    depend only on present inputs. <strong>Memory is feedback</strong>: route a circuit's
    output back into its input and the loop can sustain itself. The simplest case is
    the SR latch — two NOR (or NAND) gates feeding each other — which holds whatever
    state it was last set to: the bit remembers. Add a clock signal (a wire toggling
    billions of times per second) and gates that only update on the clock's edge, and
    you get flip-flops: one stable, synchronised bit per device. Line up eight and you
    have a register; bank billions and you have RAM and cache. The clock is the
    metronome of the whole machine — "3.2 GHz" means 3.2 billion synchronised ticks
    per second — and every register in every chip updates on those ticks, which is
    why faster clocks (and why the race for them) shaped fifty years of processor
    design.
  </p>

  <h2>The NAND miracle and the abstraction stack</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the result that explains the industry's shape: <strong>every Boolean
    function can be built from NAND alone</strong>. NOT A is NAND(A,A); A AND B is
    NOT(NAND(A,B)); A OR B is NAND(NOT A, NOT B) — De Morgan again. Since every circuit
    is combinations of AND, OR, NOT (any truth table can be written as a sum of
    products), and each of those is NANDs in a hat, NAND is universal — and so is NOR.
    Manufacturing consequence: a chip fab perfects <em>one</em> gate type with perfect
    regularity and builds everything from it; a modern processor is billions of
    transistors (tens of billions on current designs) arranged as gates, gates as
    adders, registers, memories, and a processor that executes machine code. The stack
    — transistors → gates → adders/registers → processor → machine code → your app —
    is abstraction in its purest engineering form: each layer hides the one below and
    offers a simpler contract to the one above. You have now walked it from both ends.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked derivation: requirements to circuit</h2>
  <p>
    Run the full pipeline on a realistic requirement: "The alarm should ring if the
    door opens OR the window opens, but only while the system is armed, and never
    during maintenance." Translate to variables: D (door), W (window), A (armed),
    M (maintenance). Translate to logic: ring = (D OR W) AND A AND (NOT M). Build the
    circuit: D and W feed an OR; M feeds a NOT; D+W, A and NOT-M feed a three-input
    AND (two ANDs chained). Verify against the truth-table discipline — check the
    rows that matter: armed and door opens with M=0 → ring; armed but M=1 → silent;
    disarmed → silent whatever the sensors do. Now the engineering step:
    simplify if possible (this one is already minimal — four gates). Then amend as
    requirements always do: "add a test button that forces the alarm regardless" —
    ring = (D OR W) AND A AND (NOT M) OR T, one new wire and one OR, and a new
    boundary test (T with everything else off). This is digital logic design in
    miniature: words → variables → expression → gates → table-check → amendment.
    Every real chip spec, at every scale, is this loop running patiently.
  </p>

  <h2>Equality, selection, routing: XNOR and friends</h2>
  <p>
    Three more gate arrangements earn their fame. <strong>XNOR</strong> (XOR then NOT)
    is the <em>equality</em> detector — 1 when inputs match — and equality testing is
    half of computing: comparing two 32-bit numbers is 32 XNORs feeding one big AND
    (all bits must match). <strong>Multiplexers</strong> are data switches: many data
    inputs, one selected by a control input, forwarded to the output — the traffic
    controller that lets a processor route results among registers. <strong>Decoders</strong>
    take an n-bit number and activate exactly one of 2ⁿ output lines — the "one of
    many" selector behind memory addressing (this bit pattern means row 5 → row 5's
    line lights). Put the pieces together and the processor's executive function
    appears: instructions are numbers; a decoder recognises the instruction; multiplexers
    route the operands; the adder (or its arithmetic cousins) computes; equality
    gates decide branches. None of it is mysterious any more — it is the same six
    gates, arranged with intent.
  </p>

  <h2>Why voltage, and why binary</h2>
  <p>
    A closing question deserves its answer: why build everything from two voltage
    levels? Because the physical world is analog and noisy — a "high" wire might carry
    3.2 volts or 3.4, and after a metre of copper, less. Binary design draws two wide
    bands ("anything under 0.8 volts reads as 0; anything over 2.0 reads as 1") and
    every gate <em>regenerates</em> its output to a clean level: noise that creeps in is
    squeezed out at every stage, so signals can chain millions of gates deep without
    decaying. An analog computer would accumulate every millivolt of error; a digital
    one corrects continuously. Add that two states are cheap and reliable to build in
    silicon (a transistor is, at heart, a very good switch), and binary stops looking
    like a limitation — it is the engineering choice that makes the abstraction stack
    physically possible.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>How many rows does the truth table of a 3-input circuit need, and why does the number grow the way it does?</li>
    <li>NOT(A AND B) equals which expression: (NOT A) AND (NOT B), or (NOT A) OR (NOT B)? Give one row that decides it.</li>
    <li>In a half adder, which gate produces the sum digit and which produces the carry — and why?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Eight: each input doubles the combinations, so 2 to the
    power 3 is 8 rows (000 through 111) — exponential growth, which is exactly why
    engineers compress tables back into algebra. (2) (NOT A) OR (NOT B) — De Morgan's law.
    The deciding row: A = 1, B = 0 makes NOT(A AND B) true (they are not both 1), while
    (NOT A) AND (NOT B) is false, because B is 1. (3) XOR gives the sum digit and AND
    gives the carry: 1 + 1 in binary is 10 — the sum digit is 0 when inputs match, and the
    carry is 1 only when both inputs are 1.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A gate is a tiny voltage-controlling circuit with a contract; its truth table is that contract in full.</li>
    <li>OR is inclusive — the "but not both" gate is XOR, the single most common table error.</li>
    <li>De Morgan: "not both" equals "at least one isn't" — the identity behind NAND's universality.</li>
    <li>Simplify before building: equivalent circuits have identical truth tables with fewer gates.</li>
    <li>Arithmetic is wiring: XOR makes the sum digit, AND makes the carry, chained adders make a word.</li>
    <li>Memory is feedback — a latch is two gates holding each other's state.</li>
    <li>Every Boolean function is buildable from NAND alone, and binary's wide noise bands are what make the abstraction stack physically possible.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Programming Fundamentals climbs to the next layer of the abstraction stack you just
    walked — machine code up to high-level language — and the sibling lesson on searching
    and sorting algorithms shows what that arithmetic hardware is for. The foundational
    lesson on the growth of functions supplies the ruler those algorithms are measured in.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four notes from the expert shelf. <strong>Shannon's 1937 thesis</strong> is one of
    the most consequential student papers ever written: it showed switching circuits
    <em>are</em> Boolean algebra, giving electrical engineering a mathematics and logic
    a machine — the quote-worthy fact is that the thesis also contained the first
    circuit for binary addition. <strong>Karnaugh maps</strong>: the standard tool for
    simplifying expressions of up to four variables — a grid arrangement of the truth
    table where simplifications show as adjacent rectangles; beyond four variables,
    the Quine–McCluskey algorithm (and modern logic-synthesis software) takes over.
    <strong>Propagation delay</strong>: real gates take nanoseconds to switch, so
    circuit depth is time — the reason ripple-carry adders are slow, the reason clock
    speed has physical limits (a signal must settle before the next tick), and the
    birthplace of pipelining. <strong>Moore's law</strong>: transistor counts doubling
    roughly every two years from the 1960s to the 2010s is the economic engine that
    turned the abstraction stack into phones — and its slowdown since is why the
    industry now buys speed with parallelism (more cores, more chips) rather than
    faster clocks. The gates, in every sense, still run everything.
  </p>
